      en: "Franceville, the capital of the Haut-Ogooué province, is located in southeastern Gabon along the banks of the Mpassa River. Founded in 1849 by explorer Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, it was originally intended as a settlement for freed slaves, much like Libreville. Today, Franceville is primarily known as the terminus of the Trans-Gabon Railway, which links the city to the capital and facilitates the transport of raw materials. The region is rich in mineral resources, especially manganese, which is mined in the nearby town of Moanda. Franceville is home to Masuku University and significant medical research centers, making it an academic hub for the country. The surrounding area features the Poubara Falls and scenic plateaus, offering attractive natural landmarks. The city plays a strategic role in the national economy and serves as a vital gateway to Gabon's eastern provinces.",
